    "case_id": "540",
    "case_summary": "A 60-year-old woman with:- Medical history: diabetes mellitus since 2007 (on insulin, HbA1C 8%), hypertension on lisinopril (5 mg daily), and Graves' disease- No history of pancreatic insufficiency or bowel disorders- No gastrointestinal symptoms- Presented with sudden onset of chest pain\n\nInitial presentation showed:- Mental status: Initially normal, later developed altered mental status- Vital signs were not explicitly mentioned in the case report\n\nLaboratory Tests:- Initial D-dimer: 270 ng/mL (normal <318 ng/mL)- Initial Troponins: 0.01 ng/mL (normal range 0.01–0.02 ng/mL)- Later findings:   - Anion gap: 25  - Serum beta hydroxybutyrate: 7.8 mmol/L  - Serum creatinine peaked at 6.2 mg/dL  - eGFR decreased to 7 mL/min/1.73 m²  - Potassium: 5.9 mmol/L- Urinalysis: Showed urinary tract infection- Urine culture: Grew Klebsiella oxytocaImaging Studies:- Echocardiogram: Showed moderate-sized pericardial effusion with early diastolic collapse of the right atrium- Transesophageal echocardiogram: Ruled out endocarditisPathology:- Renal biopsy findings:  - Acute tubular injury with focal tubular necrosis  - Multifocal oxalate crystal deposits  - Diffuse nodular diabetic glomerulosclerosis  - No evidence of acute interstitial nephritis  - Interstitial fibrosis tubular atrophy of 20% of sampled cortex",
